# forge9-mods

Mods for RPF9 archives, built and applied with [forge9](https://github.com/nineforge0/forge9).

Every mod here is a small `mod.toml` of **patches**: an entry path, the SHA-256 of the original
payload, and edit operations (XML value, exact text find/replace, bytes at an offset). The mods
contain no game files. You apply them to your own copy, and forge9 refuses to apply one to a
different version of the file or to a file that is already modded.

> **Status: experimental.** forge9 builds the modded archive and checks it entry by entry, but
> nobody has shown yet that the game loads a repacked archive. Back up the original files first.

## Scope and provenance

This repository holds **patches only**: an entry path, the SHA-256 of the original payload, and
edit operations. It contains no game files, assets, or copyrighted content, and none may be
contributed (CI rejects anything but manifests, READMEs and small text). A patch is applied by
you, locally, to your own legally obtained copy of the software; nothing here reproduces or
distributes that software. This project is independent and is not affiliated with, authorized
by, or endorsed by any game publisher. Use it only with files you are lawfully permitted to use.

## Use a mod

```bash
# forge9: https://github.com/nineforge0/forge9 (cargo build --release)
forge9 mod verify mods/xml-tweak/mod.toml --archive /path/to/common.rpf
forge9 mod apply  mods/xml-tweak/mod.toml --archive /path/to/common.rpf -o common.rpf.modded
forge9 diff /path/to/common.rpf common.rpf.modded     # shows exactly which entries changed
```

`verify` writes nothing: it checks the base hash and every patch precondition. A `base_sha256
mismatch` means your file is a different version, or already modded.

## Mods

| Mod | Archive | What it does |
|---|---|---|
| [xml-tweak](mods/xml-tweak) | `common.rpf` | Raises one navigation value in a vehicle points definition (example of the format) |

## Make a mod

1. Extract the entry: `forge9 extract common.rpf -o out --select 9` (the file is named by its
   offset, e.g. `out/00124150.xml`), then edit a copy.
2. Turn the edit into patch ops: `forge9 mod make --path 9 out/00124150.xml edited.xml >> mod.toml`.
   On stderr it reports how many original bytes the ops carry; keep that small.
3. Check it: `forge9 mod verify mod.toml --archive common.rpf`.
4. Open a PR adding `mods/<name>/mod.toml` and a short `README.md`.

The format is specified in [forge9's docs/mod_format.md](https://github.com/nineforge0/forge9/blob/main/docs/mod_format.md).
See [CONTRIBUTING.md](CONTRIBUTING.md) for the rules.

